      "instance_name": "racial_segregation_in_public_spaces",
      "label": "Racial Segregation in Public Spaces",
      "description": "The meme references the historical and ongoing practice of racial segregation in public spaces, such as grocery stores, where physical dividers or policies may be used to separate people based on race. This is a real-world social issue rooted in systemic racism and historical discrimination against Black people in the United States and other countries. The phrase 'white people putting a divider between your groceries' implies a racially motivated act of exclusion or separation, which is a form of microaggression or institutionalized racism.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_e76e3977"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_575cca7a",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "anti_black_stereotype",
      "label": "Anti-Black Stereotype",
      "description": "The meme relies on the stereotype that Black people are inferior or less than human, and that they are subjected to racial segregation or discrimination in mundane, everyday settings. This stereotype is reinforced by using Drake’s image — a Black celebrity — to represent the experience of being 'divided' or 'segregated' by white people. The meme implies that Black people are being treated as less than human or as objects to be separated, which is a form of racial dehumanization.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_e76e3977"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
